      "writtenOn": "2024-02-01T22:51:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do you mean you don\u0027t want to process interrupts while in any state except ST_IDLE?  Can you disable the interrupt until you read the ST_IDLE?",
      "parentUuid": "870dd7b5_55944843",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 987,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 987,
        "endChar": 28
      },
      "revId": "f1db6927e6d040b9704d8ab098040761fee04441",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "61035899_fbb8787d",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/usbc/pdc_rts54xx.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 987,
      "author": {
        "id": 1149894
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-02T00:53:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "From the realtek documentation, the sequence of commands sent to the PDC should not be interrupted, for example, 1: write command, 2: read ping status, 3: read optional data. An interrupt can fire in between the sequence that results in an I2C call to ARA. To prevent this, I let the interrupt fire and handle it after any command sequence finishes. I think keeping interrupts on and just handling the interrupt at an appropriate time is safer than disabling/enabling interrupts.",
      "parentUuid": "ff72aed1_0e2d0237",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 987,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 987,
        "endChar": 28
      },
      "revId": "f1db6927e6d040b9704d8ab098040761fee04441",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d982dd3a_d2f7e002",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/usbc/pdc_rts54xx.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 987,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-02T16:01:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t like that ST_IRQ is effectively an empty state for one of the ports.  The IRQ flow is difficult to follow, and processing in a state adds up to an extra 10ms latency.  It\u0027s also random as to which port ends up processing the interrupt and sending the `get_ara()`,\n\nI don\u0027t agree that enabling/disabling interrupts is less safe. All you need to do is disable the interrupt during init, enable the interrupt in ST_IDLE entry, and disable the interrupt in ST_WRITE entry.",
      "parentUuid": "61035899_fbb8787d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 987,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 987,
        "endChar": 28
      },
      "revId": "f1db6927e6d040b9704d8ab098040761fee04441",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
